Every data fetch lives in exactly one of three states at any moment:

- **LOADING** — the request is in flight. Show a spinner or skeleton so the user knows something is happening.
- **EMPTY** — the request succeeded but returned no items. Show a friendly message ("No results yet — add one!") rather than a blank list.
- **ERROR** — the request failed. Show a human-readable message; never let it silently crash or leave a blank screen.

Conditional rendering handles all three. Track the state with two pieces of state — `isLoading` (boolean) and `error` (string | null) — alongside your data. Then return the right JSX for each branch:

```jsx
if (isLoading) return <Spinner />;
if (error)     return <p>Something went wrong: {error}</p>;
if (!items.length) return <p>Nothing here yet.</p>;
return <ItemList items={items} />;
```

Order matters: check loading first, then error, then empty, then the happy path.
